# DO THIS FIRST {-}

Copy and paste these two lines into your R Console to install the current positpal, then **restart R** (*Session → Restart R*, or the restart button in the Console) before anything else. **Do not include them as a code chunk.**

```
install.packages("remotes")
remotes::install_url("https://quantitative.bio/downloads/positpal.tar.gz", upgrade = "never")
```

After the restart, paste this line, replacing `FILENAME.Rmd` with the name you are going to use for the .R or .Rmd file you are going to upload (rename the template first, e.g. `Group_#_CC9.Rmd`, and use that exact name):

```
positpal::start("FILENAME.Rmd")
```

Before you run it: create or open an R project folder for this challenge so that it is your working directory, then save this file in that folder under the name you will upload, and check that autosave is on (*File → Auto Save*). Closing the laptop or quitting Positron does not lose your log: when you reopen the project folder, positpal picks up where you left off and says so in the Console after your first command. **Every time you open Positron to work on this file, run the `start()` line again**, even if positpal says it picked up where you left off; it continues the same log and never erases it. See OnQ and use the positpal chatbot if you are having trouble with positpal.

# Part 1 — Import and inspect {-}

The data file is `data/oats.csv`: the yield of oats in a split-plot field trial. The field was divided into six blocks; each block was divided into three subplots, each sown with one of three oat varieties; and each subplot was split into four strips that received four rates of nitrogen fertilizer, measured on each strip. The subplots are labelled a, b and c within each block. One row is one strip. The instructions describe the trial and the variables. **Do not edit the file by hand.**

So far every model has treated each row as an independent observation. This week the rows come in groups within groups, and you will fit models that know it.

As in earlier challenges, the tasks tell you **what** to produce, not which function to use. The starter lines in some chunks are commented out so that the blank template knits; remove the `#` as you fill them in.

# Import and inspect the data

Import the data as an object called `oats` and check its structure. Make sure each column has the right type for how you will use it. Count the strips in each combination of block and variety.

```{r import-data}
oats <- read.csv("/Users/annagoldfarb/Desktop/BIOL343/BIOL343-CC/CC9/data/oats.csv")
str(oats)
```

